    <span class="section-label">Honest comparison</span>
    <h1>AI website builders vs. <em>a custom-coded site.</em></h1>
    <p>"Build your business website in 30 seconds with AI." That is the promise of Wix AI, Squarespace AI, Hostinger AI, Durable, GoDaddy AIO, and the dozen others currently advertising on every podcast you listen to. Here is what they actually deliver, what they do not, and which kind of business each is right for.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:980px">

    <h2 style="margin-top:0">What AI builders actually do</h2>
    <p>The pitch is "AI generates your website." The reality is more boring: AI builders take a small amount of business info (industry, name, location), pick a template, populate it with a generic copy generator, and sometimes generate stock-style imagery. The output is a Wix or Squarespace site you did not have to lay out yourself.</p>
    <p>What they are good at:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Speed of first draft.</strong> A "this is what your site could look like" preview in 60 seconds. Useful for inspiration, even if you never use the output.</li>
      <li><strong>Removing the blank-page problem.</strong> If you have stalled because you do not know where to start, an AI starter is a real unblock.</li>
      <li><strong>Free or near-free entry tier.</strong> Most AI builders give you the first version free; you pay when you publish or use a custom domain.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What they do not do (and quietly hide)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The output is generic by design.</strong> AI builders train on existing template content. The copy reads like every other AI-builder copy because it is. Your "About" page sounds like every plumber's About page. Your service pages sound like every electrician's service pages. Generic copy ranks worse on Google over time, not better.</li>
      <li><strong>Page speed is the same as the underlying platform.</strong> Wix AI sites get Wix performance (50&ndash;70 mobile PageSpeed). Squarespace AI gets Squarespace performance (60&ndash;75). The "AI" branding does not change the runtime; it just changes how the template was filled in.</li>
      <li><strong>Local SEO is shallow.</strong> AI-generated schema is rarely accurate. AI-generated NAP (name, address, phone) often pulls outdated public data. AI-generated service-area pages are exactly the doorway pages Google penalizes.</li>
      <li><strong>You do not own anything.</strong> The output is locked into the platform that generated it. There is no "export to your own host" path. Quitting the AI builder means starting over.</li>
      <li><strong>The "AI" is mostly a marketing layer.</strong> Most AI builders are wrappers around the same template engine they had two years ago, with a chatbot on top. The underlying code, hosting, and architecture are unchanged.</li>
      <li><strong>The trial is the trap.</strong> Free for two weeks, then $30&ndash;$50/month forever. Cancel and the site disappears. The "save" path almost always requires paying to keep the work you have already done.</li>
    </ul>

