    <h2>The honest answer</h2>
    <p><strong>An AI builder is fine for a 1-day "do I even know what I want?" experiment.</strong> If you have never built a website and do not know what one should look like, generating a draft for free is a useful research step.</p>
    <p><strong>An AI builder is the wrong answer for a real, customer-facing small-business website.</strong> The output is generic by construction; generic loses to specific in local search and in conversion. The AI saves you the layout work but it cannot save you the work of actually understanding your business and your customers &mdash; and that is the work that makes a site bring in calls.</p>
    <p><strong>"Good enough" is a trap, not a goal.</strong> An AI builder gives you good-enough. Good-enough sites do not win local search; they tie or lose to whichever competitor put in the work. If you are paying real money on Google Ads or counting on inbound calls, "good enough" costs more than a custom build over any reasonable time horizon.</p>
